When evaluating the **Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)** for a Salesforce solution with a core application and an online portal, it's important to look beyond license costs. TCO includes all costs over the lifetime of the solution—typically 3–5 years.

A useful way to present it is by breaking costs into categories.

| Cost Category         | What to Consider                                                |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Software Licensing    | Salesforce licenses, Experience Cloud licenses, add-on products |
| Implementation        | Initial design, development, testing, deployment                |
| Infrastructure        | Hosting (if external portal), storage, networking               |
| Integration           | APIs, middleware, ERP/CRM integrations                          |
| Security & Compliance | Identity management, encryption, audit, compliance              |
| Operations            | Monitoring, support, incident management                        |
| Maintenance           | Enhancements, bug fixes, Salesforce releases                    |
| Training              | User onboarding, admin training, documentation                  |
| Business Costs        | Productivity gains/losses, adoption, process improvements       |

## 1. Licensing Costs

Typically the largest recurring cost.

Include:

* Internal Salesforce user licenses
* Experience Cloud licenses
* Salesforce Shield (if required)
* Additional storage
* API limits (if applicable)
* Third-party AppExchange applications

Example:

```text
250 Internal Users
+
5,000 Customer Portal Users
+
Shield
+
DocuSign
+
Backup Solution

= Annual licensing cost
```

---

## 2. Implementation Costs

One-time costs such as:

* Business analysis
* Solution architecture
* Development
* Configuration
* Data migration
* Testing
* Deployment
* Project management

These are often significant during the first year.

---

## 3. Hosting & Infrastructure

This depends on the architecture.

### Experience Cloud

Usually includes:

* Salesforce hosting
* High availability
* Platform scaling
* Disaster recovery

Additional costs may include:

* Custom domains
* CDN (if applicable)

### Custom Portal

You may need to budget for:

* AWS/Azure/GCP hosting
* Load balancers
* Databases
* Web servers
* Backup infrastructure
* Disaster recovery
* SSL certificates
* Monitoring tools

---

## 4. Integration Costs

Rarely does Salesforce operate in isolation.

Common integrations include:

* ERP (SAP, Oracle)
* Finance systems
* Payment gateways
* Identity providers (Azure AD, Okta)
* Marketing platforms
* Data warehouses

Consider:

* API development
* Middleware licensing (e.g., MuleSoft, Boomi)
* API management
* Ongoing integration support

---

## 5. Security & Compliance

Security often has both implementation and operational costs.

Examples:

* Multi-factor authentication
* Single Sign-On
* Encryption
* Data Loss Prevention
* Audit logging
* Penetration testing
* Compliance (GDPR, HIPAA, ISO 27001, etc.)

---

## 6. Data Management

Include costs for:

* Data migration
* Data cleansing
* Data quality tools
* Backup and restore
* Data archiving
* Storage growth over time

As data volumes grow, storage-related costs may increase.

---

## 7. Operations & Support

Ongoing operational costs include:

* Salesforce administrators
* Support desk
* Monitoring
* Incident response
* Performance tuning
* User administration
* Release management

These continue throughout the solution's life.

---

## 8. Maintenance & Enhancements

Salesforce delivers platform updates multiple times each year.

Plan for:

* Regression testing
* Updating integrations
* Enhancing Flows and Apex
* New business requirements
* Technical debt remediation

---

## 9. Training & Change Management

Budget for:

* End-user training
* Administrator training
* Support documentation
* Communication plans
* Adoption initiatives

Even a technically successful implementation can underperform if users are not well supported.

---

## 10. Business Value and Indirect Costs

TCO should also consider indirect impacts such as:

* Reduced manual effort
* Faster case resolution
* Increased sales productivity
* Improved customer self-service
* Reduced call center volume
* Faster onboarding

These factors influence the overall return on investment (ROI).

---

